---
// Hand-authored inline SVG: how 11217 walks up the Urbanist Atlas region
// graph. Inline (not an <img>) so it inherits the Broadsheet tokens from
// global.css and needs no client JS — mermaid, which draws this same shape
// in the Atlas repo's docs/region-graph.md, can't run under `script-src 'self'`.
//
// Nodes and edges are transcribed from the shipped seed data, not from the
// design doc:
//   brooklyn  parents = ["nyc", "ny"]        api/seed/regions_us.toml
//   nyc       parents = ["nyc-metro"]        api/seed/regions_us.toml
//   nyc-metro parents = ["nyc-tristate"]     api/seed/regions_us_msas.toml
//   ny / nj / nyc-tristate  parents = []     states + multistate seeds
//
// nyc-metro also carries rollup_states = ["nj", "ny"], deliberately not drawn:
// it's browse-only and never walked during a lookup, so an edge would imply
// exactly the relationship this diagram exists to rule out.
//
// Styles live in global.css (.post-diagram) per the one-stylesheet rule.
---

<figure class="post-figure post-diagram">
  <svg
    viewBox="0 0 680 400"
    role="img"
    aria-labelledby="dg-nyc-title dg-nyc-desc"
    class="dg"
  >
    <title id="dg-nyc-title">The region graph above ZIP code 11217</title>
    <desc id="dg-nyc-desc">
      A directed graph. ZIP code 11217 anchors at Brooklyn. Brooklyn has two
      parent edges: one to New York City, and a second direct edge to New York
      State. New York City's parent is New York Metro, whose parent is the
      Tri-State Region. New York State is a top-level node with no parent, so
      Brooklyn reaches it without passing through the metro. New Jersey is also
      a top-level node, but nothing in Brooklyn's ancestry connects to it, so
      New Jersey's advocacy groups are never returned.
    </desc>

    <defs>
      <marker
        id="dg-nyc-arrow"
        viewBox="0 0 10 10"
        refX="9"
        refY="5"
        markerWidth="6"
        markerHeight="6"
        orient="auto-start-reverse"
      >
        <path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 z" class="dg-arrowhead" />
      </marker>
    </defs>

    {/* ---- edges (drawn first so node fills sit on top) ---- */}
    <g class="dg-edges" fill="none" marker-end="url(#dg-nyc-arrow)">
      {/* Brooklyn -> New York City */}
      <path class="dg-edge" d="M114 316 V 274" />
      {/* New York City -> New York Metro */}
      <path class="dg-edge" d="M114 222 V 174" />
      {/* New York Metro -> Tri-State Region */}
      <path class="dg-edge" d="M114 122 V 74" />
      {/* Brooklyn -> New York State, the second parent edge */}
      <path class="dg-edge dg-edge--key" d="M214 340 H 340 V 74" />
    </g>

    <text x="226" y="358" class="dg-note dg-note--start">direct edge — skips the metro</text>

    {/* ---- top-level nodes ---- */}
    <g class="dg-group">
      <rect x="14" y="22" width="200" height="48" class="dg-node" />
      <text x="114" y="42" class="dg-label">Tri-State Region</text>
      <text x="114" y="59" class="dg-sub">Tri-State Transportation Campaign</text>
    </g>

    <g class="dg-group">
      <rect x="250" y="22" width="180" height="48" class="dg-node" />
      <text x="340" y="42" class="dg-label">New York State</text>
      <text x="340" y="59" class="dg-sub">New York Bicycling Coalition</text>
    </g>

    <g class="dg-group dg-group--out">
      <rect x="466" y="22" width="200" height="48" class="dg-node dg-node--out" />
      <text x="566" y="42" class="dg-label">New Jersey</text>
      <text x="566" y="59" class="dg-sub">Bike JC · NJ Bike &amp; Walk</text>
    </g>
    <text x="566" y="90" class="dg-note">never reached from 11217</text>

    {/* ---- the metro chain ---- */}
    <g class="dg-group">
      <rect x="14" y="122" width="200" height="48" class="dg-node" />
      <text x="114" y="142" class="dg-label">New York Metro</text>
      <text x="114" y="159" class="dg-sub">TransitCenter</text>
    </g>

    <g class="dg-group">
      <rect x="14" y="222" width="200" height="48" class="dg-node" />
      <text x="114" y="242" class="dg-label">New York City</text>
      <text x="114" y="259" class="dg-sub">Transportation Alternatives</text>
    </g>

    {/* ---- the anchor ---- */}
    <g class="dg-group">
      <rect x="14" y="316" width="200" height="48" class="dg-node dg-node--anchor" />
      <text x="114" y="336" class="dg-label">Brooklyn</text>
      <text x="114" y="353" class="dg-sub">Bike South Brooklyn</text>
    </g>
    <text x="114" y="386" class="dg-call">11217 anchors here</text>
  </svg>

  <figcaption>
    A lookup for 11217 walks up from Brooklyn and collects everything above it.
    Brooklyn's second edge goes straight to New York State rather than up
    through the metro — which is what keeps New Jersey's groups out of a
    Brooklyn search while still surfacing the genuinely regional ones.
  </figcaption>
</figure>
